<article class="env-proposition" id="A9CCE0">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/A9CCE0">Proposition <span data-tag="A9CCE0">6.2.3.7</span>.</a> Let $\calX $ be a semitopos. </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
Any equivalence $X \rightarrow Y$ in $\calX $ is an effective epimorphism. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
If $f,g: X \rightarrow Y$ are homotopic morphisms in $\calX $, then $f$ is an effective epimorphism if and only if $g$ is an effective epimorphism. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
If $F: \calY \rightarrow \calX $ is a left exact presentable functor between semitopoi, and $f: U \rightarrow X$ is an effective epimorphism in $\calX $, then $F(f)$ is an effective epimorphism in $\calY $. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assertions $(1)$ and $(2)$ are obvious. To prove $(3)$, we observe that $f$ is an effective epimorphism if and only if it can be extended to an augmented simplicial object $U_{\bullet }$ which is both a simplicial resolution and a Čech nerve. Since $F$ is left exact, it preserves the property of being a Čech nerve; since $F$ preserves colimits, it preserves the property of being a simplicial resolution. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>